  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What makes surface ocean currents?",
      "a": "Mainly steady winds dragging on the water, like the trade winds and the westerlies. Earth's spin and the shapes of the continents then steer the currents into giant loops.",
      "asks": [
        "How does wind move the ocean?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is a gyre?",
      "a": "A gyre is a huge loop of ocean currents circling an ocean basin, driven by winds and Earth's spin. There are five main gyres: in the North and South Atlantic, the North and South Pacific, and the Indian Ocean.",
      "asks": [
        "What is the North Atlantic Gyre?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is the Great Pacific Garbage Patch?",
      "a": "An area in the calm middle of the North Pacific gyre where floating plastic collects, carried there by the circling currents. It is mostly tiny bits of plastic, not a solid island.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does plastic collect in the ocean?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do currents curve?",
      "a": "Earth spins under the moving water, which makes currents curve to the right in the northern hemisphere and to the left in the southern. This is the Coriolis effect; continents also block and turn them.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does the Gulf Stream turn?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How do we know where currents go?",
      "a": "Scientists track drifting buoys and use satellites. Spilled cargo has helped too: plastic bath toys lost from a ship in the Pacific in 1992 later washed up on beaches far away.",
      "asks": [
        "What were the rubber ducks lost at sea?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "In 1992 a ship lost about 28,000 plastic bath toys in the Pacific, and their drift helped scientists study ocean currents.",
    "There are five major ocean gyres."
